WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.085 --> 00:00:03.640
Why did prescription opioids bring so much misery

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:06.660
to the small towns of post-industrial America?

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.520
The standard narrative is to put all the blame on OxyContin,

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:16.000
a powerful painkiller pushed on rural America by the profiteers at Purdue Pharma.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.290
Under a settlement agreement,

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:21.610
Purdue would plead guilty to three federal criminal charges related to its role

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.380
in pushing the powerful painkiller OxyContin.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:29.800
In this telling, the opioid epidemic is a morality tale of capitalism run amok,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.620
and regulation made toothless by anti-government zealots.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:40.540
Sally Satel, a practicing psychiatrist and resident scholar at the American Enterprise Institute,

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.800
has a more complicated story to tell.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.620
In 2018, she moved to Ironton, Ohio, a small economically depressed town in Appalachia.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.540
With the help of "Hillbilly Elegy" author J.D. Vance,

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:55.970
she found a job working with patients and social service providers

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:58.763
to better understand the opioid epidemic.

00:00:58.763 --> 00:01:03.040
Satel says that the opioid crisis didn't start with OxyContin,

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.830
it was a natural outgrowth of a century-old tradition

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:12.570
of medicating pain as a way of tending to the broken bodies of the region's laborers.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:16.310
All the men worked in these very physically brutal jobs,

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.840
and if they couldn't work, they couldn't live there.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.590
Even though many of them were in agony, they had to work,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.260
and the coal camp doctor, who was employed by the company,

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.810
had to get them into the mines, so they medicated them so that they could work.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:36.870
Which is not to say that the medication might not have been indicated alone for pain control,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.800
but it also is a way to make sure that the labor continued.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.460
There had been trafficking of prescription pills for a long time, gray market pills,

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.078
mainly Percocet, Lortab, Vicodin, or oxycodone.

00:01:50.078 --> 00:01:54.600
Satel stresses that when the Purdue salesforce came to small towns in Appalachia,

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.110
it was pushing on open clinic doors.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.090
Their product, OxyContin,

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:02.510
was a particularly potent form of oxycodone.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:06.460
Your average Percocet is five to 10 milligrams of oxycodone,

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.000
but an OxyContin pill can have up to 80 milligrams.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.470
But there was a reason for that potency.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:17.833
The drug was engineered to release the medication slowly over a longer period of time.

00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:22.250
The appeal of OxyContin is that it was long-acting so that if you had chronic pain,

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:23.790
you had a more steady blood level.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.310
That is a pharmacologically legitimate strategy.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.500
But patients started misusing it.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.040
When you chop up that pill and either snort it or inject it,

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.680
mix it with water and inject it,

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.620
then you're getting an enormous rush, and it's pharmaceutical grade.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:43.940
The common narrative is that patients with no history of addiction got duped

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:48.120
into becoming addicts after doctors prescribed them pain medication,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.790
that's mostly a myth.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.444
Only 22 percent got them from a doctor.

00:02:52.444 --> 00:02:56.740
And even that doesn't mean that they did not present themselves in such a way

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:02.270
as to get medication when they really were intending to abuse it, which is not that uncommon.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:06.510
The average person who abuses these medications knows what they're doing.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:11.360
Just as the original formulation of OxyContin didn't create the opioid crisis,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.060
removing it from the market didn't make the problem go away.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.760
Pill prescribing peaked around 2010, 2011.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.530
And that peak, or the descent after that peak, really was an amalgam of several things.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.880
One, OxyContin was reformulated so it couldn't be chopped up,

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.500
but as pills became less available to people who abused them, heroin was there.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:41.513
2015 is when you start to see the rise of heroin, and it was really always waiting in the wings.

00:03:41.513 --> 00:03:46.530
Satel also challenges the view that addiction is a disease similar to diabetes

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:47.540
or Alzheimer's.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:53.040
That narrative, she says, denies the agency of individuals with substance abuse issues

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.180
and makes it harder to treat them.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.560
Of course the brain is changed in addiction.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.640
But the point is, the brain isn't changed

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.200
to the point where a person can no longer make decisions.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.170
It's a behavioral phenomenon that responds to contingencies,

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:09.850
it responds to consequences.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.610
If you talk to someone who drinks too much

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:17.857
or uses drugs too much, and I emphasize the too much because that's the problem, 

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:22.420
and I'd say to them, "Why are you doing that? What's going on?"

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.650
That question makes sense.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.490
That question can be answered in existential terms.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.087
If I said to the person with Alzheimer's disease,

00:04:30.087 --> 00:04:32.010
"Why do you have Alzheimer's disease?"

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:37.350
Well, maybe they'll talk to me about plaques and tangles and that kind of neuropathology,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:41.670
but the answer doesn't come in the form in existential language.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:45.810
And that's very important, because that goes to why people use

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.685
and it goes to how we get them out of it.

00:04:47.685 --> 00:04:49.470
Satel argues that substance abuse

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.000
derives from both inborn predilections and environment.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.430
Effective treatment has to deal with both factors.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.978
I think the more we medicalize this problem,

00:04:58.978 --> 00:05:00.780
the more we're going to be misled.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.479
The more we're going to put emphasis on the medications,

00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:06.397
the methadones, the buprenorphine, the naltrexones,

00:05:06.397 --> 00:05:08.546
and I'm all in favor of those medications,

00:05:08.546 --> 00:05:10.970
because they help people stand still.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:13.830
And if you're out, if you're craving, if you're running around,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:17.290
you're never going to make the first step towards stopping.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:19.420
So, I'm all for the medications,

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:25.340
but to think that could possibly be enough strikes me as incredibly naive.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.140
The National Institute on Drug Abuse,

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:30.810
they show brain scans and they talk about this as, quote,

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.670
"a disease like any other," which of course it's not.

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:37.550
And when you go to a place like Ironton,

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.020
you could see how medicine and public health are necessary

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:47.050
but not at all sufficient because what do you do when you finally do get somebody sober

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:50.180
but they're in an environment that doesn't appear to offer much?

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:51.760
In a deeply ironic way,

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.350
many of the people who blame the opioid epidemic

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:58.540
on bad pills see the solution as different set of pills.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:01.630
Methadone, buprenorphine, and naltrexone.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:07.250
Satel stresses that the best way forward is to give individuals tools to make better use decisions

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.620
while improving their chances to live lives

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:11.360
with open-ended futures.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.290
Sometimes a pill does make a massive difference,

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:18.370
but usually so much other work needs to be done as well.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.020
But I think having a pill,

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:24.410
that, quote, unquote, "magic bullet," the hope of the fantasy of a magic bullet,

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.083
can reinforce that notion.

